{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KhCAxcFEv72SSx9Nac8Qhp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tpackage pose\n2\t\n3\timport (\n4\t\t\"bytes\"\n5\t\t\"context\"\n6\t\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n7\t\t\"encoding/hex\"\n8\t\t\"fmt\"\n9\t\t\"io\"\n10\t\t\"os\"\n11\t\t\"sort\"\n12\t\t\"strings\"\n13\t\t\"sync\"\n14\t\t\"sync/atomic\"\n15\t)\n16\t\n17\t// The common operation layer (spec pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff).\n18\t//\n19\t// An MCP route is one of five things, and which one decides how it may share code\n20\t// with the CLI:\n21\t//\n22\t//   - domain-direct: the adapter calls the domain package;\n23\t//   - cli-wrapper: the adapter asks the CLI to run (a process, or, for a read-only\n24\t//     operation whose semantics the CLI package owns, the same function in process);\n25\t//   - read-only-planning: the adapter returns a plan that writes nothing;\n26\t//   - admitted-write: the adapter reaches a write point of the domain, which\n27\t//     revalidates authority, assurance, policy and subjects itself;\n28\t//   - external-handoff: the operation belongs to another actor or surface; the\n29\t//     adapter returns a typed handoff and executes nothing.\n30\t//\n31\t// stdio is not an IPC channel shared between clients, and sharing code never lends\n32\t// one transport the identity, signature or human presence of another.\n33\t\n34\t// RouteClass names how an MCP route relates to the CLI.\n35\ttype RouteClass string\n36\t\n37\tconst (\n38\t\tRouteDomainDirect    RouteClass = \"domain-direct\"\n39\t\tRouteCLIWrapper      RouteClass = \"cli-wrapper\"\n40\t\tRouteReadOnlyPlan    RouteClass = \"read-only-planning\"\n41\t\tRouteAdmittedWrite   RouteClass = \"admitted-write\"\n42\t\tRouteExternalHandoff RouteClass = \"external-handoff\"\n43\t)\n44\t\n45\t// InProcessCommand is a CLI command whose semantics the CLI package owns and that\n46\t// is read-only and bounded, so an adapter may run it in process instead of\n47\t// spawning the binary.\n48\ttype InProcessCommand func(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int\n49\t\n50\tvar (\n51\t\tinProcessMu       sync.RWMutex\n52\t\tinProcessCommands = map[string]InProcessCommand{}\n53\t\n54\t\t// cliSpawnAttempts counts the times a wrapper tried to start the POSE binary:\n55\t\t// the evidence that an extracted operation no longer does (R1, R9).\n56\t\tcliSpawnAttempts atomic.Int64\n57\t\t// cliInProcessRuns counts the runs served in process.\n58\t\tcliInProcessRuns atomic.Int64\n59\t\t// inProcessSlots bounds the in-process runs alive at once, abandoned ones\n60\t\t// included: a run that outlives its deadline keeps its slot until it finishes,\n61\t\t// so repeated timeouts cannot accumulate work beyond the slots (R1).\n62\t\tinProcessSlots = make(chan struct{}, inProcessMaxRuns)\n63\t)\n64\t\n65\t// inProcessMaxRuns is the number of in-process runs a server keeps alive at once.\n66\tconst inProcessMaxRuns = 4\n67\t\n68\t// RegisterInProcessCommand lets the CLI package offer a read-only command to the\n69\t// wrappers of this package. Registering a command that writes is a defect: the\n70\t// wrapper gives it no process boundary and no way to be killed.\n71\tfunc RegisterInProcessCommand(name string, run InProcessCommand) {\n72\t\tinProcessMu.Lock()\n73\t\tdefer inProcessMu.Unlock()\n74\t\tinProcessCommands[name] = run\n75\t}\n76\t\n77\tfunc inProcessCommand(name string) (InProcessCommand, bool) {\n78\t\tinProcessMu.RLock()\n79\t\tdefer inProcessMu.RUnlock()\n80\t\trun, ok := inProcessCommands[name]\n81\t\treturn run, ok\n82\t}\n83\t\n84\t// CLISpawnAttempts and CLIInProcessRuns report the counters for measurement.\n85\tfunc CLISpawnAttempts() int64 { return cliSpawnAttempts.Load() }\n86\tfunc CLIInProcessRuns() int64 { return cliInProcessRuns.Load() }\n87\t\n88\t// inProcessRunsTheSpawnedEngine reports whether running a command in this process\n89\t// answers as the spawn of the installed binary would. It does not when POSE_EXECUTABLE\n90\t// names another binary, or when the file this process started from has been replaced\n91\t// since (`pose update` writes the new engine at the same path): the spawned run would\n92\t// use the updated engine, and a running server holds the old one in memory (spec\n93\t// pose-mcp-server-survives-self-update R2, which the in-process route must not break).\n94\t// A test process has no installed binary to compare with and runs in process.\n95\tfunc inProcessRunsTheSpawnedEngine() bool {\n96\t\tif os.Getenv(\"POSE_EXECUTABLE\") != \"\" {\n97\t\t\treturn false\n98\t\t}\n99\t\tif executableAtStartErr != nil || strings.HasSuffix(executableAtStart, \".test\") || executableInfoAtStart == nil {\n100\t\t\treturn true\n101\t\t}\n102\t\tnow, err := os.Stat(executableAtStart)\n103\t\tif err != nil {\n104\t\t\treturn false // the installed binary is gone: the spawn reports it, a stale engine does not\n105\t\t}\n106\t\treturn os.SameFile(executableInfoAtStart, now)\n107\t}\n108\t\n109\t// runInProcess runs a registered read-only command with the same contract runCLI\n110\t// gives a spawned one: a non-zero exit is an error carrying the command's message,\n111\t// and a deadline or cancellation is an execution failure with no verdict, never a\n112\t// failed result. A command cannot be killed, so a stopped run leaves its goroutine\n113\t// to finish; registered commands are read-only and bounded, and the disclosure says so.\n114\tfunc (s Store) runInProcess(ctx context.Context, run InProcessCommand, args []string) ([]byte, error) {\n115\t\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, cliTimeout)\n116\t\tdefer cancel()\n117\t\tfailure := func(cleanup string) error {\n118\t\t\treturn &GateExecutionError{Command: \"pose \" + strings.Join(args, \" \"), Cause: executionCause(ctx), Cleanup: cleanup}\n119\t\t}\n120\t\t// Cancelled before starting: nothing runs.\n121\t\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n122\t\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the command did not start; nothing ran and nothing was written\")\n123\t\t}\n124\t\t// Admission: a bounded number of runs, abandoned ones counted. A caller that cannot\n125\t\t// get a slot before its context ends has an execution failure, not a verdict.\n126\t\tselect {\n127\t\tcase inProcessSlots <- struct{}{}:\n128\t\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n129\t\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the command did not start: every in-process slot is held by a run still finishing\")\n130\t\t}\n131\t\ttype outcome struct {\n132\t\t\tstdout, stderr []byte\n133\t\t\tcode           int\n134\t\t}\n135\t\t// A caller that was cancelled while it waited for a slot does not start the command.\n136\t\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n137\t\t\t<-inProcessSlots\n138\t\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the command did not start; nothing ran and nothing was written\")\n139\t\t}\n140\t\tdone := make(chan outcome, 1)\n141\t\tgo func() {\n142\t\t\tdefer func() { <-inProcessSlots }()\n143\t\t\tvar stdout, stderr bytes.Buffer\n144\t\t\t// A panic in the command must not take the server down, as the exit of a spawned\n145\t\t\t// process never did: it becomes a failed run with the panic as its message.\n146\t\t\tcode := func() (code int) {\n147\t\t\t\tdefer func() {\n148\t\t\t\t\tif r := recover(); r != nil {\n149\t\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&stderr, \"panic: %v\", r)\n150\t\t\t\t\t\tcode = 2\n151\t\t\t\t\t}\n152\t\t\t\t}()\n153\t\t\t\treturn run(s.Root, args[1:], &stdout, &stderr)\n154\t\t\t}()\n155\t\t\tdone <- outcome{stdout.Bytes(), stderr.Bytes(), code}\n156\t\t}()\n157\t\tvar res outcome\n158\t\tselect {\n159\t\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n160\t\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the read-only command was running in this process and finishes in the background; it wrote nothing\")\n161\t\tcase res = <-done:\n162\t\t}\n163\t\t// A completion that races a cancellation is not a verdict: the caller asked to stop.\n164\t\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n165\t\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the read-only command finished after the cancellation; its result was discarded and it wrote nothing\")\n166\t\t}\n167\t\tcliInProcessRuns.Add(1)\n168\t\tif res.code != 0 {\n169\t\t\tmsg := strings.TrimSpace(string(res.stderr))\n170\t\t\tif msg == \"\" {\n171\t\t\t\tmsg = strings.TrimSpace(string(res.stdout))\n172\t\t\t}\n173\t\t\tif msg == \"\" {\n174\t\t\t\tmsg = fmt.Sprintf(\"exit status %d\", res.code)\n175\t\t\t}\n176\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"pose %s failed: %s\", args[0], msg)\n177\t\t}\n178\t\treturn res.stdout, nil\n179\t}\n180\t\n181\t// OperationHandoff is the typed description of an operation another actor or\n182\t// surface performs (spec pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff R5). It names\n183\t// what to run as structured arguments and what it was computed from; it executes\n184\t// nothing, carries no secret, is not an approval, and does not retarget itself:\n185\t// when the inputs it names are no longer current, the operation must be planned\n186\t// again.\n187\ttype OperationHandoff struct {\n188\t\tOperation string `json:\"operation\"`\n189\t\t// RequestID identifies the handoff: the same operation, scope and plan give the\n190\t\t// same id, so a resumption can be matched to the plan it came from.\n191\t\tRequestID string `json:\"request_id\"`\n192\t\tScope     string `json:\"scope\"`\n193\t\tPhase     string `json:\"phase\"`\n194\t\t// Argv is the CLI invocation, one element per argument. It is not a shell line.\n195\t\tArgv []string `json:\"argv\"`\n196\t\t// RequiredInputs are the arguments the performer must still supply, that the handoff\n197\t\t// does not carry because they name the performer: the apply refuses to run a step\n198\t\t// without them and never invents them.\n199\t\tRequiredInputs []string `json:\"required_inputs,omitempty\"`\n200\t\t// Inputs bind the handoff to the state it was computed from.\n201\t\tInputs HandoffInputs `json:\"inputs\"`\n202\t\t// RequiredAuthority says who may perform it; the handoff grants none.\n203\t\tRequiredAuthority string `json:\"required_authority\"`\n204\t\t// Executes is always false: the adapter that returned the handoff did not run it.\n205\t\tExecutes bool `json:\"executes\"`\n206\t\t// Target is the project the plan was computed in, by its logical id and the digest of\n207\t\t// its root, never by a path: the MCP contract exposes no filesystem root. The handoff\n208\t\t// does not retarget itself: the performer applies it there, after verifying that context.\n209\t\tTarget HandoffTarget `json:\"target\"`\n210\t\t// ContextRequirement says what the performer verifies before applying: the\n211\t\t// adapter never infers a context revision from a context it merely presented\n212\t\t// (R3), and `pose close --apply` takes no context flag, only the plan digest.\n213\t\tContextRequirement string `json:\"context_requirement\"`\n214\t\t// Receipt says how the outcome comes back: the CLI result and `pose close\n215\t\t// --resume` read the state again.\n216\t\tReceipt string `json:\"receipt\"`\n217\t\t// Invalidation says when this handoff stops being valid.\n218\t\tInvalidation string `json:\"invalidation\"`\n219\t}\n220\t\n221\t// HandoffTarget names the project an MCP caller selected: its logical id, and the digest\n222\t// of the root the plan was read from when the read view recorded one. A path is never\n223\t// carried (spec pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff, review round 6).\n224\ttype HandoffTarget struct {\n225\t\tProjectID  string `json:\"project_id,omitempty\"`\n226\t\tRootDigest string `json:\"root_digest,omitempty\"`\n227\t}\n228\t\n229\t// HandoffInputs are the digests a handoff was computed from.\n230\ttype HandoffInputs struct {\n231\t\tPlanDigest   string `json:\"plan_digest\"`\n232\t\tSpecDigest   string `json:\"spec_digest\"`\n233\t\tRevision     string `json:\"revision\"`\n234\t\tPolicyDigest string `json:\"policy_digest\"`\n235\t\tGeneration   string `json:\"generation,omitempty\"`\n236\t}\n237\t\n238\t// CloseoutHandoff describes `pose close <scope> --apply` for a plan that has steps\n239\t// left, or nil when nothing is left to apply. The plan is the one the read-only\n240\t// planner computed; this function reads it and writes nothing.\n241\tfunc CloseoutHandoff(plan CloseoutPlan, target HandoffTarget) *OperationHandoff {\n242\t\tif plan.Terminal || plan.Scope == \"\" || len(plan.Blockers) > 0 {\n243\t\t\treturn nil\n244\t\t}\n245\t\tif plan.ReadView != nil && plan.ReadView.Freshness == ReadViewFreshnessUnknown {\n246\t\t\treturn nil // inputs not proven stable: the plan must be recomputed, not applied\n247\t\t}\n248\t\tpending, authority, needsReviewer := false, \"maintainer\", false\n249\t\tfor _, step := range plan.Steps {\n250\t\t\tswitch step.State {\n251\t\t\tcase CloseoutStateBlocked:\n252\t\t\t\treturn nil // a blocked step stops the apply: there is nothing to hand off\n253\t\t\tcase CloseoutStatePending, CloseoutStateWaiting:\n254\t\t\t\tpending = true\n255\t\t\t}\n256\t\t\tif step.ID == CloseoutStepMechanical && (step.State == CloseoutStatePending || step.State == CloseoutStateWaiting) {\n257\t\t\t\tneedsReviewer = true\n258\t\t\t}\n259\t\t\tif step.ID == CloseoutStepJudgment && step.State == CloseoutStateWaiting {\n260\t\t\t\tauthority = \"reviewer (a different-actor judgment is pending; the apply stops there)\"\n261\t\t\t}\n262\t\t}\n263\t\tif !pending {\n264\t\t\treturn nil\n265\t\t}\n266\t\tsum := sha256.Sum256([]byte(\"close\\x00\" + plan.Scope + \"\\x00\" + plan.Digest))\n267\t\thandoff := &OperationHandoff{\n268\t\t\tOperation: \"close\",\n269\t\t\tRequestID: \"hnd-\" + hex.EncodeToString(sum[:8]),\n270\t\t\tScope:     plan.Scope,\n271\t\t\tPhase:     \"closeout\",\n272\t\t\tArgv:      []string{\"pose\", \"close\", plan.Scope, \"--apply\", \"--digest\", plan.Digest},\n273\t\t\tInputs: HandoffInputs{\n274\t\t\t\tPlanDigest: plan.Digest, SpecDigest: plan.Inputs.SpecDigest, Revision: plan.Inputs.Revision, PolicyDigest: plan.Inputs.PolicyDigest,\n275\t\t\t},\n276\t\t\tTarget:             target,\n277\t\t\tRequiredAuthority:  authority,\n278\t\t\tContextRequirement: \"run it in the root of the target project, after `pose context --task \" + plan.Scope + \" --json` there shows the authority and revision this plan was computed under; `pose close --apply` takes no context flag, its binding is the plan digest, and this handoff carries no context\",\n279\t\t\tReceipt:            \"the CLI result of the apply, then `pose close \" + plan.Scope + \" --resume` or `pose_closeout_state` with include_plan to read the state again\",\n280\t\t\tInvalidation:       \"any change to the spec, the policy or the revision makes the plan digest stale; the index generation it was read at is recorded in inputs.generation for the performer to compare, and the apply does not check it; plan again instead of applying this handoff\",\n281\t\t}\n282\t\tif needsReviewer {\n283\t\t\t// The mechanical attestation names the reviewing execution; `pose close --apply`\n284\t\t\t// stops at that step without --reviewer, so the handoff says it is owed.\n285\t\t\thandoff.RequiredInputs = append(handoff.RequiredInputs, \"--reviewer <agent:id|human:id> (names the reviewing execution; the performer supplies it, the apply never invents it)\")\n286\t\t}\n287\t\tif plan.ReadView != nil && len(plan.ReadView.Bindings) > 0 {\n288\t\t\thandoff.Inputs.Generation = plan.ReadView.Bindings[0].IndexGeneration\n289\t\t\tif handoff.Target.RootDigest == \"\" {\n290\t\t\t\thandoff.Target.RootDigest = plan.ReadView.Bindings[0].RootDigest\n291\t\t\t}\n292\t\t}\n293\t\treturn handoff\n294\t}\n295\t\n296\t// RouteInventory is the declared class of every MCP tool. A tool missing from it,\n297\t// or listed here and not advertised, fails TestEveryMCPRouteHasADeclaredClass: the\n298\t// inventory cannot drift from the catalog (R1).\n299\ttype RouteInventory map[string]RouteClass\n300\t\n301\t// SortedTools returns the tools of an inventory in order.\n302\tfunc (r RouteInventory) SortedTools() []string {\n303\t\tout := make([]string, 0, len(r))\n304\t\tfor name := range r {\n305\t\t\tout = append(out, name)\n306\t\t}\n307\t\tsort.Strings(out)\n308\t\treturn out\n309\t}\n310\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"448af3a3-1f58-451a-a2f7-9c6ed8d2b4f9","timestamp":"2026-11-09T06:36:21.861Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3984980499/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/pose/common_operation.go","content":"package pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"sync/atomic\"\n)\n\n// The common operation layer (spec pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff).\n//\n// An MCP route is one of five things, and which one decides how it may share code\n// with the CLI:\n//\n//   - domain-direct: the adapter calls the domain package;\n//   - cli-wrapper: the adapter asks the CLI to run (a process, or, for a read-only\n//     operation whose semantics the CLI package owns, the same function in process);\n//   - read-only-planning: the adapter returns a plan that writes nothing;\n//   - admitted-write: the adapter reaches a write point of the domain, which\n//     revalidates authority, assurance, policy and subjects itself;\n//   - external-handoff: the operation belongs to another actor or surface; the\n//     adapter returns a typed handoff and executes nothing.\n//\n// stdio is not an IPC channel shared between clients, and sharing code never lends\n// one transport the identity, signature or human presence of another.\n\n// RouteClass names how an MCP route relates to the CLI.\ntype RouteClass string\n\nconst (\n\tRouteDomainDirect    RouteClass = \"domain-direct\"\n\tRouteCLIWrapper      RouteClass = \"cli-wrapper\"\n\tRouteReadOnlyPlan    RouteClass = \"read-only-planning\"\n\tRouteAdmittedWrite   RouteClass = \"admitted-write\"\n\tRouteExternalHandoff RouteClass = \"external-handoff\"\n)\n\n// InProcessCommand is a CLI command whose semantics the CLI package owns and that\n// is read-only and bounded, so an adapter may run it in process instead of\n// spawning the binary.\ntype InProcessCommand func(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int\n\nvar (\n\tinProcessMu       sync.RWMutex\n\tinProcessCommands = map[string]InProcessCommand{}\n\n\t// cliSpawnAttempts counts the times a wrapper tried to start the POSE binary:\n\t// the evidence that an extracted operation no longer does (R1, R9).\n\tcliSpawnAttempts atomic.Int64\n\t// cliInProcessRuns counts the runs served in process.\n\tcliInProcessRuns atomic.Int64\n\t// inProcessSlots bounds the in-process runs alive at once, abandoned ones\n\t// included: a run that outlives its deadline keeps its slot until it finishes,\n\t// so repeated timeouts cannot accumulate work beyond the slots (R1).\n\tinProcessSlots = make(chan struct{}, inProcessMaxRuns)\n)\n\n// inProcessMaxRuns is the number of in-process runs a server keeps alive at once.\nconst inProcessMaxRuns = 4\n\n// RegisterInProcessCommand lets the CLI package offer a read-only command to the\n// wrappers of this package. Registering a command that writes is a defect: the\n// wrapper gives it no process boundary and no way to be killed.\nfunc RegisterInProcessCommand(name string, run InProcessCommand) {\n\tinProcessMu.Lock()\n\tdefer inProcessMu.Unlock()\n\tinProcessCommands[name] = run\n}\n\nfunc inProcessCommand(name string) (InProcessCommand, bool) {\n\tinProcessMu.RLock()\n\tdefer inProcessMu.RUnlock()\n\trun, ok := inProcessCommands[name]\n\treturn run, ok\n}\n\n// CLISpawnAttempts and CLIInProcessRuns report the counters for measurement.\nfunc CLISpawnAttempts() int64 { return cliSpawnAttempts.Load() }\nfunc CLIInProcessRuns() int64 { return cliInProcessRuns.Load() }\n\n// inProcessRunsTheSpawnedEngine reports whether running a command in this process\n// answers as the spawn of the installed binary would. It does not when POSE_EXECUTABLE\n// names another binary, or when the file this process started from has been replaced\n// since (`pose update` writes the new engine at the same path): the spawned run would\n// use the updated engine, and a running server holds the old one in memory (spec\n// pose-mcp-server-survives-self-update R2, which the in-process route must not break).\n// A test process has no installed binary to compare with and runs in process.\nfunc inProcessRunsTheSpawnedEngine() bool {\n\tif os.Getenv(\"POSE_EXECUTABLE\") != \"\" {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif executableAtStartErr != nil || strings.HasSuffix(executableAtStart, \".test\") || executableInfoAtStart == nil {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tnow, err := os.Stat(executableAtStart)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false // the installed binary is gone: the spawn reports it, a stale engine does not\n\t}\n\treturn os.SameFile(executableInfoAtStart, now)\n}\n\n// runInProcess runs a registered read-only command with the same contract runCLI\n// gives a spawned one: a non-zero exit is an error carrying the command's message,\n// and a deadline or cancellation is an execution failure with no verdict, never a\n// failed result. A command cannot be killed, so a stopped run leaves its goroutine\n// to finish; registered commands are read-only and bounded, and the disclosure says so.\nfunc (s Store) runInProcess(ctx context.Context, run InProcessCommand, args []string) ([]byte, error) {\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, cliTimeout)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\tfailure := func(cleanup string) error {\n\t\treturn &GateExecutionError{Command: \"pose \" + strings.Join(args, \" \"), Cause: executionCause(ctx), Cleanup: cleanup}\n\t}\n\t// Cancelled before starting: nothing runs.\n\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the command did not start; nothing ran and nothing was written\")\n\t}\n\t// Admission: a bounded number of runs, abandoned ones counted. A caller that cannot\n\t// get a slot before its context ends has an execution failure, not a verdict.\n\tselect {\n\tcase inProcessSlots <- struct{}{}:\n\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the command did not start: every in-process slot is held by a run still finishing\")\n\t}\n\ttype outcome struct {\n\t\tstdout, stderr []byte\n\t\tcode           int\n\t}\n\t// A caller that was cancelled while it waited for a slot does not start the command.\n\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n\t\t<-inProcessSlots\n\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the command did not start; nothing ran and nothing was written\")\n\t}\n\tdone := make(chan outcome, 1)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tdefer func() { <-inProcessSlots }()\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr bytes.Buffer\n\t\t// A panic in the command must not take the server down, as the exit of a spawned\n\t\t// process never did: it becomes a failed run with the panic as its message.\n\t\tcode := func() (code int) {\n\t\t\tdefer func() {\n\t\t\t\tif r := recover(); r != nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&stderr, \"panic: %v\", r)\n\t\t\t\t\tcode = 2\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}()\n\t\t\treturn run(s.Root, args[1:], &stdout, &stderr)\n\t\t}()\n\t\tdone <- outcome{stdout.Bytes(), stderr.Bytes(), code}\n\t}()\n\tvar res outcome\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the read-only command was running in this process and finishes in the background; it wrote nothing\")\n\tcase res = <-done:\n\t}\n\t// A completion that races a cancellation is not a verdict: the caller asked to stop.\n\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, failure(\"the read-only command finished after the cancellation; its result was discarded and it wrote nothing\")\n\t}\n\tcliInProcessRuns.Add(1)\n\tif res.code != 0 {\n\t\tmsg := strings.TrimSpace(string(res.stderr))\n\t\tif msg == \"\" {\n\t\t\tmsg = strings.TrimSpace(string(res.stdout))\n\t\t}\n\t\tif msg == \"\" {\n\t\t\tmsg = fmt.Sprintf(\"exit status %d\", res.code)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"pose %s failed: %s\", args[0], msg)\n\t}\n\treturn res.stdout, nil\n}\n\n// OperationHandoff is the typed description of an operation another actor or\n// surface performs (spec pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff R5). It names\n// what to run as structured arguments and what it was computed from; it executes\n// nothing, carries no secret, is not an approval, and does not retarget itself:\n// when the inputs it names are no longer current, the operation must be planned\n// again.\ntype OperationHandoff struct {\n\tOperation string `json:\"operation\"`\n\t// RequestID identifies the handoff: the same operation, scope and plan give the\n\t// same id, so a resumption can be matched to the plan it came from.\n\tRequestID string `json:\"request_id\"`\n\tScope     string `json:\"scope\"`\n\tPhase     string `json:\"phase\"`\n\t// Argv is the CLI invocation, one element per argument. It is not a shell line.\n\tArgv []string `json:\"argv\"`\n\t// RequiredInputs are the arguments the performer must still supply, that the handoff\n\t// does not carry because they name the performer: the apply refuses to run a step\n\t// without them and never invents them.\n\tRequiredInputs []string `json:\"required_inputs,omitempty\"`\n\t// Inputs bind the handoff to the state it was computed from.\n\tInputs HandoffInputs `json:\"inputs\"`\n\t// RequiredAuthority says who may perform it; the handoff grants none.\n\tRequiredAuthority string `json:\"required_authority\"`\n\t// Executes is always false: the adapter that returned the handoff did not run it.\n\tExecutes bool `json:\"executes\"`\n\t// Target is the project the plan was computed in, by its logical id and the digest of\n\t// its root, never by a path: the MCP contract exposes no filesystem root. The handoff\n\t// does not retarget itself: the performer applies it there, after verifying that context.\n\tTarget HandoffTarget `json:\"target\"`\n\t// ContextRequirement says what the performer verifies before applying: the\n\t// adapter never infers a context revision from a context it merely presented\n\t// (R3), and `pose close --apply` takes no context flag, only the plan digest.\n\tContextRequirement string `json:\"context_requirement\"`\n\t// Receipt says how the outcome comes back: the CLI result and `pose close\n\t// --resume` read the state again.\n\tReceipt string `json:\"receipt\"`\n\t// Invalidation says when this handoff stops being valid.\n\tInvalidation string `json:\"invalidation\"`\n}\n\n// HandoffTarget names the project an MCP caller selected: its logical id, and the digest\n// of the root the plan was read from when the read view recorded one. A path is never\n// carried (spec pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff, review round 6).\ntype HandoffTarget struct {\n\tProjectID  string `json:\"project_id,omitempty\"`\n\tRootDigest string `json:\"root_digest,omitempty\"`\n}\n\n// HandoffInputs are the digests a handoff was computed from.\ntype HandoffInputs struct {\n\tPlanDigest   string `json:\"plan_digest\"`\n\tSpecDigest   string `json:\"spec_digest\"`\n\tRevision     string `json:\"revision\"`\n\tPolicyDigest string `json:\"policy_digest\"`\n\tGeneration   string `json:\"generation,omitempty\"`\n}\n\n// CloseoutHandoff describes `pose close <scope> --apply` for a plan that has steps\n// left, or nil when nothing is left to apply. The plan is the one the read-only\n// planner computed; this function reads it and writes nothing.\nfunc CloseoutHandoff(plan CloseoutPlan, target HandoffTarget) *OperationHandoff {\n\tif plan.Terminal || plan.Scope == \"\" || len(plan.Blockers) > 0 {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif plan.ReadView != nil && plan.ReadView.Freshness == ReadViewFreshnessUnknown {\n\t\treturn nil // inputs not proven stable: the plan must be recomputed, not applied\n\t}\n\tpending, authority, needsReviewer := false, \"maintainer\", false\n\tfor _, step := range plan.Steps {\n\t\tswitch step.State {\n\t\tcase CloseoutStateBlocked:\n\t\t\treturn nil // a blocked step stops the apply: there is nothing to hand off\n\t\tcase CloseoutStatePending, CloseoutStateWaiting:\n\t\t\tpending = true\n\t\t}\n\t\tif step.ID == CloseoutStepMechanical && (step.State == CloseoutStatePending || step.State == CloseoutStateWaiting) {\n\t\t\tneedsReviewer = true\n\t\t}\n\t\tif step.ID == CloseoutStepJudgment && step.State == CloseoutStateWaiting {\n\t\t\tauthority = \"reviewer (a different-actor judgment is pending; the apply stops there)\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !pending {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tsum := sha256.Sum256([]byte(\"close\\x00\" + plan.Scope + \"\\x00\" + plan.Digest))\n\thandoff := &OperationHandoff{\n\t\tOperation: \"close\",\n\t\tRequestID: \"hnd-\" + hex.EncodeToString(sum[:8]),\n\t\tScope:     plan.Scope,\n\t\tPhase:     \"closeout\",\n\t\tArgv:      []string{\"pose\", \"close\", plan.Scope, \"--apply\", \"--digest\", plan.Digest},\n\t\tInputs: HandoffInputs{\n\t\t\tPlanDigest: plan.Digest, SpecDigest: plan.Inputs.SpecDigest, Revision: plan.Inputs.Revision, PolicyDigest: plan.Inputs.PolicyDigest,\n\t\t},\n\t\tTarget:             target,\n\t\tRequiredAuthority:  authority,\n\t\tContextRequirement: \"run it in the root of the target project, after `pose context --task \" + plan.Scope + \" --json` there shows the authority and revision this plan was computed under; `pose close --apply` takes no context flag, its binding is the plan digest, and this handoff carries no context\",\n\t\tReceipt:            \"the CLI result of the apply, then `pose close \" + plan.Scope + \" --resume` or `pose_closeout_state` with include_plan to read the state again\",\n\t\tInvalidation:       \"any change to the spec, the policy or the revision makes the plan digest stale; the index generation it was read at is recorded in inputs.generation for the performer to compare, and the apply does not check it; plan again instead of applying this handoff\",\n\t}\n\tif needsReviewer {\n\t\t// The mechanical attestation names the reviewing execution; `pose close --apply`\n\t\t// stops at that step without --reviewer, so the handoff says it is owed.\n\t\thandoff.RequiredInputs = append(handoff.RequiredInputs, \"--reviewer <agent:id|human:id> (names the reviewing execution; the performer supplies it, the apply never invents it)\")\n\t}\n\tif plan.ReadView != nil && len(plan.ReadView.Bindings) > 0 {\n\t\thandoff.Inputs.Generation = plan.ReadView.Bindings[0].IndexGeneration\n\t\tif handoff.Target.RootDigest == \"\" {\n\t\t\thandoff.Target.RootDigest = plan.ReadView.Bindings[0].RootDigest\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn handoff\n}\n\n// RouteInventory is the declared class of every MCP tool. A tool missing from it,\n// or listed here and not advertised, fails TestEveryMCPRouteHasADeclaredClass: the\n// inventory cannot drift from the catalog (R1).\ntype RouteInventory map[string]RouteClass\n\n// SortedTools returns the tools of an inventory in order.\nfunc (r RouteInventory) SortedTools() []string {\n\tout := make([]string, 0, len(r))\n\tfor name := range r {\n\t\tout = append(out, name)\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(out)\n\treturn out\n}\n","numLines":310,"startLine":1,"totalLines":310}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KhCAxcFEv72SSx9Nac8Qhp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
